laurel 2870: scanout owner + fbcon off the panel. Owner bug 2026-11-01 07:45 (GL, SMMU stage 1, 2104 in): "the kernel log flashes ABOVE the iOS UI when you move or do anything" (white text on black). Cause [V, code + boot-args]: the laurel boot-args have serial=3 and the ADT has /sweet-fbcon (mkadt, reg 0x5c000000 720x1560), so arm_init switches the kernel console to the serial ops (cons_ops_index = SERIAL_CONS_OPS; 'Serial keyboard started' in the logs) and every printf/IOLog character goes _cnputs -> serial_putc_options -> uart_putc -> fbcon_transmit_data, which draws white (0xffffffff) on black (0xff000000) 2x glyphs and clears 2 rows per newline straight into the boot framebuffer 0x5c000000. That is AppleCLCD's surface 1: backboardd's GL copies every other frame into it (redwood-gl '1 picture copied out') and since 2104 the MDP scans it directly ('LAUREL FB HANDOFF: swap 2 shows surface 1 ... by scanning it directly'). Each kernel line during the UI was drawn into the frame on the panel until the next surface-1 frame overwrote it; more activity = more kernel lines = more text. The video console (2104's shadow) gets no text at all with serial=3, so 2104's self-check passed while the real console drew on the panel. Fix: (1) fbcon: AppleCLCD start moves it into the console shadow (laurel_fbcon_retarget; line LAUREL FBCON: on), the vsync copies the shadow into the boot console frames when the fbcon drew (laurel_fbcon_take_dirty: the boot log is live again after AppleCLCD start); at the first iOS frame a fbcon still on the boot framebuffer is muted; a panic always draws, cleans the rows and points the MDP at the buffer (laurel_fbcon_show_hook -> laurel_fb_panic_show). (2) scanout owner: scanFrom (the only writer of the pipe's SRC0_ADDR) gets what the buffer holds (iOS frame, black, kernel text, panic); the console shadow is always kernel text, a flip buffer holds what it was last filled with (fFlipHolds); after the first iOS frame a kernel-text scanout is refused (the panel keeps its frame), logged once per caller with the caller address (LAUREL FB OWNER: refused: ...), except in a panic. (3) once the first iOS frame is latched: the console shadow and both flip buffers (those not on the panel) are cleared to black (LAUREL FB OWNER: on: ...); the copy path also clears a back buffer that still holds kernel text. Kill switch fbowner=0 (no refusal, no clear; the fbcon move stays).

